      {
        "id": "ex-empirical-risk-sgd-oracle-is-unbiased",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "Uniform empirical-risk sampling gives an unbiased bounded-variance gradient",
        "deps": [
          "alg-minibatch-stochastic-gradient-descent",
          "thm-stochastic-direction-expected-average-stationarity",
          "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-probability-measure",
          "def-measure",
          "def-random-element-and-real-random-variable",
          "def-filtration-and-filtered-probability-space",
          "def-adapted-and-integrable-stochastic-process",
          "def-conditional-expectation-as-an-ae-class",
          "def-conditional-variance",
          "lem-conditional-variance-is-well-defined-and-has-the-second-moment-formula",
          "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "def-derivative"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "statement": "Assume AC for the conditional-expectation stationarity bound below. Take instance space $(\\{*\\},2^{\\{*\\}})$, action and label spaces $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$, the population law $P_{\\mathrm{pop}}=\\delta_{(*,0)}$, the nonempty predictor class $\\mathcal F=\\{h_\\theta:\\theta\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ with $h_\\theta(*)=\\theta$, and squared loss $\\ell(a,y)=\\frac12(a-y)^2$. Fix an ordered sample $S=((*,y_1),\\ldots,(*,y_m))$ of integer length $m\\ge1$ with $y_i\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\bar y=m^{-1}\\sum_i y_i$ and $v_S=m^{-1}\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2$. Fix a deterministic initial parameter $\\theta_1\\in\\mathbb R$, a constant stepsize $0<\\alpha\\le1$, an integer batch size $b\\ge1$, and an integer horizon $T\\ge1$. On the finite uniform probability space $(\\Omega,2^\\Omega,Q)$, where $\\Omega=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}^{bT}$ and each point has $Q$-mass $m^{-bT}$, write $I_{t,j}$ for the coordinate indices, independently uniform with replacement, for $1\\le t\\le T$ and $1\\le j\\le b$. Set $\\mathcal F_t=\\sigma(I_{s,j}:1\\le s<t,\\ 1\\le j\\le b)$ for $1\\le t\\le T+1$, so $\\mathcal F_1$ is trivial. At each iteration $t=1,\\ldots,T$, average the component gradients to form $G_t=b^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^b(\\theta_t-y_{I_{t,j}})$ and set $\\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\alpha G_t$. For the fixed-sample empirical risk $\\widehat R_S(h_\\theta)$, this direction has conditional mean $\\mathbb E_Q[G_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=\\theta_t-\\bar y=\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\widehat R_S(h_{\\theta_t})$ and conditional variance $\\operatorname{Var}_Q(G_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t)=v_S/b$. The objective has $1$-Lipschitz gradient and global infimum $v_S/2$, so the expected-average theorem yields\n$$\\frac1T\\sum_{t=1}^T\\mathbb E_Q\\left|\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\widehat R_S(h_{\\theta_t})\\right|^2\\le\\frac{\\alpha v_S}{b}+\\frac{(\\theta_1-\\bar y)^2}{T\\alpha}.$$\nThe sampling law here is over the fixed empirical sample; no population-risk gradient identity is asserted."
      },
